@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmt:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/traditions/> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#
# prima-materia - the Maya ritual transmission
#
# Order of the file: the tradition, then the examination in the language of
# Zuyua and the office it guards, then the ordeals and the sign left behind in
# the Popol Vuh, then the fasts, the caution of the carvers and the remedy for
# a foretold year. The instance was registered in
# traditions/registry-outside-contact.ttl and is moved here now that passages
# have been read. Why it stands at a disputed contact route stands on the
# instance below, where it survives compilation.
#

pmt:MayaRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Maya ritual transmission"@en, "Maya-Ritualüberlieferung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Maya transmission of creation, of the calendar as a moving structure of obligation, of divination by counting seeds, and of an examination in a figurative language by which the right to rule was said to be decided."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: the Chumayel, chapter IX, in the Roys translation; Landa in the Gates translation, sections XXVII, XXXVII and XL; the Popol Vuh in the Goetz and Morley version of the Recinos translation, part I chapter 2 and part II chapters 7 to 9. What was not opened: the Ritual of the Bacabs, the Dresden Codex, the Tizimin and Mani books, and Landa section XXVI. The reasons stand in the issues named in the harvest note of prima-materia#454."@en ;
    skos:note "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent, and that correction is the finding of this run rather than a formality. Absent asks whether a route was available in the period in which the corpus was fixed, and every work read here was fixed after 1524: Landa's account survives only in a later copy, the Popol Vuh only in the hand of a Dominican of 1701 to 1703, the Chumayel in a compilation signed at Chumayel in 1782. That the Chilam Balam books received European material is not a suspicion but a philological result, demonstrated for the almanac and zodiac material of the Kaua book against named Spanish printed sources. Against that stands an argument for continuity in the incantation genres, which was not decided here. See prima-materia#455 and prima-materia#456."@en ;
    skos:note "A witness of this corpus is therefore a witness for the period of its fixation and not for the period it describes. The strongest case is Landa: the autograph of 1566 is lost, what survives is an anonymous copy, and the thesis that the surviving text is a compilation of the seventeenth or eighteenth century out of Landa's papers is in the literature and not answered here. See prima-materia#457."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Popol Vuh in the Ximenez manuscript; the Books of Chilam Balam; the Dresden Codex; the Ritual of the Bacabs; Landa, Relacion de las cosas de Yucatan, as the account of an outside observer" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    dcterms:source "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 89-98" .

#
# The examination in the language of Zuyua and the office it guards
#

pmp:ZuyuaThan a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The interrogation in the language of Zuyua"@en, "Das Verhör in der Sprache von Zuyua"@de ;
    skos:definition "An examination held at the close of one katun and the opening of the next, in which the first head-chief demands of each town chief a series of objects named figuratively, and the candidate must produce the thing actually meant. The demands are answered by everyday things: the sun is a large fried egg, the brains of the sky are copal gum, the house six spans long is a very large hat, the things that stop the bottom of the cenote are white and yellow jicamas."@en ;
    skos:note "The source names the moment exactly and not vaguely: the katun that ends is 3 Ahau and the one that begins is 1 Ahau, so the procedure carries a date rather than an occasion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 89-98; the same chapter is pp. 42-50 in the first edition, Carnegie Institution of Washington Publication 438, 1933" .

pmc:ZuyuaNaat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Understanding the language of Zuyua"@en, "Das Verstehen der Sprache von Zuyua"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity to hear a figurative demand and produce the thing meant. The witness does not treat this as a skill beside descent but as the same fact: what the examination asks is whether the candidate can explain how the ruling men came and whether he is of the lineage of rulers."@en ;
    skos:note "The text says nothing about where this language is learned or whether the answers are taught. That silence is the whole difficulty of the procedure as a test and is taken up at pmc:ZuyuaExamination."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 89-93" .

pmc:PopDzam a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The mat and the throne"@en, "Die Matte und der Thron"@de ;
    skos:definition "The office of a town chief, named in the source by the two objects handed over with it, together with the first wand of office. The candidate who has understood comes before his lord on bended knees and receives them."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 92-93" .

pmc:MayaOfficePresupposesZuyuaNaat a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The mat and the throne presuppose understanding the language of Zuyua"@en, "Matte und Thron setzen das Verstehen der Sprache von Zuyua voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:PopDzam ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ZuyuaNaat ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The source states the consequence for the whole procedure and states it as death, not as exclusion: those who die are those who do not understand, those who live will understand it. The punishments are named in a sequence. The hands of those who fail are bound before them to a wooden collar and they are pulled along with the cord; they are taken to the house of the ruler; they shall be hung by the neck, the tips of their tongues shall be cut off, their eyes shall be torn out. The chapter adds an omen for the case that the chiefs of the towns do not understand: ill-omened is the star adorning the night." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is quotable and because the text sets no lesser outcome beside it. There is no passage naming a second attempt, a number of chiefs to be examined, or a remedy for failure."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 91-93" .

pmc:ZuyuaExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "The Zuyua interrogation as a test of descent"@en, "Das Zuyua-Verhör als Abstammungsprobe"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol the tradition states for itself. The claim is that the questions separate men of the ruling lineage from men who are not, and the tradition acts on the claim to the point of execution. The outcome is binary and is applied on the day."@en ;
    skos:note "What the protocol cannot survive is the document that preserves it. The manuscript that reports the examination also prints the answers, so from the moment of that writing the procedure separates those who have read this book from those who have not, and no longer those of the lineage from the rest. A test whose key is published measures access to the key. This is a property of the record and not a modern objection to the tradition, and it is the reason the state stands at procedure without cases rather than higher."@en ;
    skos:note "A route by which a European examination genre could have reached this material exists and is documented: a Yucatec translation of the Spanish question-and-answer book of the emperor Adrian and the boy Epitus stands in a colonial Yucatec manuscript. Nobody has set the Zuyua catalogue against that literature. See prima-materia#456."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:ZuyuaNaat ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded case in which a man of the ruling lineage failed the questions, or in which a man not of that lineage answered them. The tradition claims the questions decide descent, so a single clean case on either side falls due against it." ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:source "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 89-98" .

